 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of pixels or voxels, as it requires a single pass to compute differences and averages [S6, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the difference and squared difference arrays, or $O(1)$ if computed incrementally [S6, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** MSE is computationally efficient and widely supported by deep learning frameworks [S4, unofficial]. It is the standard loss for many NeRF implementations due to its simplicity and differentiability [S7, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, differentiable, and effective for optimizing pixel-wise accuracy [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Does not correlate well with human perception, especially for high-frequency details [S1, unofficial]. Sensitive to outliers and misalignments [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When pixel-wise accuracy is the primary goal, or as a baseline loss function [S4, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When perceptual quality is more important than numerical accuracy, or when dealing with significant misalignments [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of pixels/voxels compared, as it involves a single pass to compute differences and averages [S6, S8, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the intermediate squared error array, or $O(1)$ if computed incrementally [S6, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Standard baseline for NeRF and volume rendering training [S2, S4, S7, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling:** Scales linearly with resolution and batch size [S6, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, differentiable, and provides a clear monotonic error signal [S4, S7, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Does not account for structural similarity or perceptual quality, often leading to blurry results compared to SSIM or LPIPS [S1, unofficial][S7, S11, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** As a primary loss for initial training or when pixel-perfect intensity accuracy is required [S4, S7, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When perceptual quality is more important than pixel accuracy, or when dealing with high-frequency details where MSE may fail to capture structural fidelity [S1, unofficial][S7, S11, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-MSE and PSNR are standard metrics in image processing and computer vision. In the context of volume rendering and NeRF, MSE was introduced as the primary loss function in the original NeRF paper [S2, unofficial]. It has since become a standard benchmark for evaluating the quality of 3D reconstructions and novel views [S1, unofficial]. PSNR is commonly used alongside MSE to provide a logarithmic scale for error evaluation [S2, unofficial][S4, unofficial].
+MSE Loss is a fundamental statistical measure used in signal processing and machine learning for decades. In the context of volume rendering and NeRF, it was popularized by the original NeRF paper [S2, unofficial] and subsequent implementations [S4, unofficial][S7, unofficial] as the primary photometric loss. It is widely used in 3D reconstruction, CBCT reconstruction [S1, unofficial], and novel view synthesis [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
 
